# tail-toto — Implementation (C11)

## Module layout

| Path | Role |
|------|------|
| `include/tail_toto.h` | `TAIL_TOTO_VERSION_STRING`, `TAIL_TOTO_EXIT_*` enum, `enum tail_toto_mode`, `struct tail_toto_opts` |
| `include/tail_toto_emit.h` | every stderr diagnostic and warning (`tail_toto_emit_*()`) |
| `include/tail_toto_cli.h` | `TAIL_TOTO_ARG_*` flag macros, `enum tail_toto_parse_result`, `tail_toto_parse_args()`, `print_help()`, `print_version()` |
| `include/tail_toto_count.h` | `enum tail_toto_count_result`, `tail_toto_parse_count()`, `tail_toto_parse_uint()`, `tail_toto_parse_seconds()` |
| `include/tail_toto_io.h` | `TAIL_TOTO_BUFSIZE`, `TAIL_TOTO_O_BINARY`, `tail_toto_write_all()`, `tail_toto_read_retry()`, `tail_toto_set_binary_stdio()` |
| `include/tail_toto_tail.h` | `enum tail_toto_status`, `tail_toto_tail_fd()` |
| `include/tail_toto_run.h` | `tail_toto_run()` declaration |
| `src/main.c` | `main()` only — parse args, dispatch help/version, print follow-option warnings, call `tail_toto_run()` |
| `src/tail_toto_emit.c` | stderr diagnostics |
| `src/tail_toto_cli.c` | option parsing (long-option table, short clusters, obsolete form), help/version text |
| `src/tail_toto_count.c` | NUM, PID / stats and seconds parsing (no diagnostics) |
| `src/tail_toto_io.c` | `write()` / `read()` retry wrappers, Win32 binary-mode setup |
| `src/tail_toto_tail.c` | `tail_toto_tail_fd()` — seekable and stream algorithms for lines/bytes, last-N and `+N` |
| `src/tail_toto_run.c` | `tail_toto_run()` — iterate operands, open/close, headers, aggregate exit status |

Each `.c` file opens with a **chain-of-thought comment block** (responsibility,
syscalls, heap usage, C standard). Preserve that pattern when adding files.

## C standard

**ISO C11** (`-std=c11`), `-D_POSIX_C_SOURCE=200809L` for POSIX.1-2008 where
available (`open`, `read`, `write`, `lseek`, `fstat`, `close`, etc.), and
`-D_FILE_OFFSET_BITS=64` for large-file `off_t`.

Platform selection is via Makefile define **`TAIL_TOTO_WIN32_IO`**
(set when a compile probe for `<io.h>` + `_setmode(0, _O_BINARY)` succeeds).

## Options (`struct tail_toto_opts`)

```c
enum tail_toto_mode {
    TAIL_TOTO_MODE_LINES_LAST,   /* -n N   (default, N = 10) */
    TAIL_TOTO_MODE_LINES_FROM,   /* -n +N */
    TAIL_TOTO_MODE_BYTES_LAST,   /* -c N   */
    TAIL_TOTO_MODE_BYTES_FROM    /* -c +N  */
};

struct tail_toto_opts {
    enum tail_toto_mode mode;
    uintmax_t count;
    int headers;                 /* -1 auto, 0 = -q, 1 = -v */
    char delim;                  /* '\n', or '\0' with -z */
    int retry;                   /* --retry seen (warning only) */
    int pid_given;               /* --pid=PID with PID != 0 (warning only) */
    int nfiles;
    char **files;                /* points into argv; never copied */
};
```

## Argument handling (`src/main.c`, `src/tail_toto_cli.c`)

Flag macros in `tail_toto_cli.h` (`TAIL_TOTO_ARG_*`): `--help`, `--version`,
`--lines` / `-n`, `--bytes` / `-c`, `--quiet` / `--silent` / `-q`,
`--verbose` / `-v`, `--zero-terminated` / `-z`, `--sleep-interval` / `-s`,
`--pid`, `--retry`, `--max-unchanged-stats`, `--` and `-`. The parser compares
against these macros only.

`tail_toto_parse_args()` returns `enum tail_toto_parse_result`
(`RUN`, `HELP`, `VERSION`, `ERROR`); `main()` prints help/version, or on `RUN`
emits the `--retry` warning, then the PID warning, then returns 0 at once when
`count == 0` in a `*_LAST` mode (GNU opens nothing then: no headers, no open
errors), otherwise calls `tail_toto_run()`.

1. **Obsolete form** (checked first, GNU `parse_obsolete_option`): only when
   `argc == 2`, or `argc == 3` and `argv[2]` is not an option (`-` is fine), or
   `argc` is 3–4 and `argv[2]` is `--`. `argv[1]` is `+` or `-` (but not `-`
   alone or `-c` alone), optional digits (default 10), optional one of `b`
   (512-byte blocks), `c` (bytes), `l` (lines), then the end. `+` means from
   start. Digit overflow → `invalid number: '<argv1>': <strerror(ERANGE)>`;
   `b` scaling overflow → `invalid number: '<argv1>'`. Anything else falls
   through to normal parsing (a `+…` word is then a FILE).
2. **Left-to-right loop** over the remaining arguments:
   - `-`, words not starting with `-`, and everything after `--` are operands,
     compacted in place into `argv[1 …]`; with `POSIXLY_CORRECT` set the first
     operand ends option parsing
   - Long options: table in GNU order (bytes, lines, max-unchanged-stats, pid,
     quiet, retry, silent, sleep-interval, verbose, zero-terminated, help,
     version); any unique prefix matches; an exact match wins; entries with the
     same action never make each other ambiguous; value from `=VALUE` or the
     next argument
   - Short clusters (`-qvz`, `-qn5`): `n`, `c`, `s` take the rest of the token
     or the next argument; a digit → `option used in invalid context -- D`
     (no hint line)
   - `--help` / `--version` return immediately when reached
3. `-n` / `-c` value: a leading `-` is stripped; a leading `+` sets the
   **sticky** from-start flag (never cleared) and stays in the string;
   `tail_toto_parse_count()` then parses it. Mode = lines/bytes from the
   option, `*_FROM` when the sticky flag is set.
4. If no operands → treat as a single `-` operand.

## Number parsing (`src/tail_toto_count.c`)

All functions return `enum tail_toto_count_result`
(`TAIL_TOTO_COUNT_OK`, `_INVALID`, `_OVERFLOW`) and print nothing.

- `tail_toto_parse_count(s, &out)`: leading whitespace, optional `+`, decimal
  digits, optional suffix (`b`, `kB`, `K`/`k`, `KiB`, `MB`, `M`/`m`, … `Q`); a
  suffix alone means 1. Overflow → `_OVERFLOW` (an invalid suffix wins).
  Hand-rolled (no `strtoumax`) so it behaves identically on every C library.
- `tail_toto_parse_uint(s, max, &out)`: same without suffixes, bounded by `max`
  (PID: `INT_MAX`; stats: `UINTMAX_MAX`).
- `tail_toto_parse_seconds(s)`: `strtod()`; invalid when nothing converts,
  trailing characters remain, the value overflows, or it is not `>= 0` (NaN).

Error message formats (all **stderr**):

```
tail-toto: invalid option -- 'x'
Try 'tail-toto --help' for more information.

tail-toto: unrecognized option '--foo'
Try 'tail-toto --help' for more information.

tail-toto: option '--v' is ambiguous; possibilities: '--verbose' '--version'
Try 'tail-toto --help' for more information.

tail-toto: option '--quiet' doesn't allow an argument
Try 'tail-toto --help' for more information.

tail-toto: option requires an argument -- 'n'
Try 'tail-toto --help' for more information.

tail-toto: option '--lines' requires an argument
Try 'tail-toto --help' for more information.

tail-toto: option used in invalid context -- 5
tail-toto: invalid number of lines: 'abc'
tail-toto: invalid number of bytes: '1Q': Value too large for defined data type
tail-toto: invalid number: '-99999999999999999999': Numerical result out of range
tail-toto: invalid number of seconds: 'abc'
tail-toto: invalid PID: 'abc'
tail-toto: invalid maximum number of unchanged stats between opens: 'abc'
tail-toto: warning: --retry ignored; --retry is useful only when following
tail-toto: warning: PID ignored; --pid=PID is useful only when following
```

Overflow reasons use `strerror(EOVERFLOW)` (`strerror(ERANGE)` for the obsolete
form), so their text comes from the platform C library.

## Running (`tail_toto_run`)

```
tail_toto_set_binary_stdio()                       // no-op on POSIX
show = headers == 1 || (headers == -1 && nfiles > 1)
status = TAIL_TOTO_EXIT_OK
for each operand i:
    name = "-" ? "standard input" : operand
    fd   = "-" ? STDIN_FILENO : open(operand, O_RDONLY | TAIL_TOTO_O_BINARY)
    if fd < 0 → emit_open_error(name), status = ERR, continue
    if show → write "==> name <==\n" (prefixed by "\n" unless first header)
    r = tail_toto_tail_fd(fd, STDOUT_FILENO, &opts)
    r == READ_ERR  → emit_read_error(name), status = ERR
    r == WRITE_ERR → emit_write_error(), exit(ERR)
    close(fd) unless stdin
return status
```

A header is only written once the file opens successfully (GNU behaviour).
Headers always end in `'\n'`, even with `-z`.

## Tail algorithms (`tail_toto_tail_fd`)

Returns `TAIL_TOTO_OK`, `TAIL_TOTO_READ_ERR`, or `TAIL_TOTO_WRITE_ERR` with
`errno` preserved. Never exits, never prints diagnostics. Every line algorithm
uses `opts->delim` as the line delimiter.

Dispatch: `fstat()` → if `S_ISREG` and `lseek(fd, 0, SEEK_CUR)` succeeds →
seekable path; else stream path.

### Seekable, last N lines

```
end = lseek(fd, 0, SEEK_END)
if count == 0 → return OK
read chunks of TAIL_TOTO_BUFSIZE backwards from end
ignore a trailing delimiter at end-1 (it terminates the last line)
count delimiters scanning backwards; start = offset just after the Nth one
if file start reached first → start = initial offset
lseek(fd, start, SEEK_SET); stream to out_fd
```

### Seekable, last N bytes

`start = max(initial offset, end - N)`; `lseek` + stream.

### Stream, last N lines

Read fixed-size chunks into a list; track delimiter count per chunk; evict the
oldest chunk while the remaining chunks still contain more than N lines. At EOF,
skip leading surplus lines in the oldest chunk and write the rest.

### Stream, last N bytes

Ring buffer holding at most N bytes; at EOF write it out in at most two
`write` calls (wrap-around).

### `+N` modes (seekable or stream)

Skip forward: lines → consume until `N - 1` delimiters seen (`+0` ≡ `+1`);
bytes → skip `N - 1` bytes (`lseek` when seekable). Then stream the rest.

## Output

- File data and headers are emitted via **`tail_toto_write_all()`** (raw
  `write()`) — no `printf` / `puts` on this path.
- Bytes are written exactly as read; no delimiter is appended to a final
  unterminated line.
- `--help` and `--version` text may use `printf` / `fputs` to stdout.

## Exit codes

| Code | Constant | Meaning |
|------|----------|---------|
| `0` | `TAIL_TOTO_EXIT_OK` | All inputs processed; or `--help` / `--version` |
| `1` | `TAIL_TOTO_EXIT_ERR` | Usage error, any open/read failure, write failure |

## Error messages

All diagnostics on **stderr**:

- System error: `tail-toto: <context>: <strerror(errno)>`
- Open failure: `tail-toto: cannot open '<name>' for reading: <strerror(errno)>`
- Read failure: `tail-toto: error reading '<name>': <strerror(errno)>`
- Write failure: `tail-toto: write error: <strerror(errno)>`
- Usage errors and warnings: see formats above

Never print errors to stdout.

## `tail_toto_write_all()` contract

In `tail_toto_io.c`:

- **Precondition:** `buf != NULL` or `len == 0`
- **Postcondition:** returns `0` with all `len` bytes written, or `-1` with
  `errno` set
- On `EINTR`: retry immediately
- On short write: continue from the remaining offset
- Never exits; callers map `-1` to `TAIL_TOTO_WRITE_ERR`

`tail_toto_read_retry()` mirrors this: retries `EINTR`, returns bytes read,
`0` on EOF, `-1` on error.

## Naming

Public run entry point: **`tail_toto_run()`** (not bare `run`).
